# @PROJECT_NAME@

Generated Qornix Web application with Schema-driven Dynamic API enabled. The template includes a bundled first-run demo schema so a new user can apply a real schema immediately, seed data and explore queries without writing XML from scratch.

This template is intended to showcase the full schema-driven backend flow out of the box:

1. Manage XML schema safely.
2. Apply a confirmed schema plan.
3. Explore metadata-backed queries visually.
4. Browse table data.
5. Open, update or delete a single row.
6. Run API recipes.
7. Read generated OpenAPI docs and text specifications for schema XML and query payloads.

When `QORNIX_ENABLE_ASYNC_DB=ON`, the generated app registers Dynamic API CRUD routes through the async DB path. The default SQLite demo uses the explicit `sqlite_sync_offloaded` adapter; PostgreSQL/MySQL builds can use real async drivers by enabling `QORNIX_ENABLE_ASYNC_POSTGRES=ON` or `QORNIX_ENABLE_ASYNC_MYSQL=ON` and setting the database config accordingly.

## Build and run

```bash
mkdir -p build
cd build
cmake ..
cmake --build .
```

The default build creates both the raw executable and a portable deploy bundle:

```text
build/@PROJECT_NAME@
build/deploy/@PROJECT_NAME@/
```

For normal use, run the application from the deploy bundle:

```bash
cd build/deploy/@PROJECT_NAME@
./@PROJECT_NAME@
```

Open:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8008/
```

## Portable deployment

The generated project is configured to assemble a self-contained application directory during `cmake --build .`:

```text
build/deploy/@PROJECT_NAME@/
├── @PROJECT_NAME@
├── config.yaml
├── README.md
├── templates/
├── static/
├── schema/
│   ├── app.schema.xml
│   └── schema_app.xsd
├── doc/
└── logs/
```

You can copy the whole `build/deploy/@PROJECT_NAME@` directory to another directory on the same machine, or to another Linux machine that has compatible runtime shared libraries installed. The app discovers its root at runtime in this order:

```text
QORNIX_APP_ROOT environment variable
directory containing the executable
parent of the executable directory
current working directory
parent of current working directory
compile-time source directory as a development fallback
```

This means these launch styles are supported:

```bash
# Run from inside the bundle
cd build/deploy/@PROJECT_NAME@
./@PROJECT_NAME@

# Run by absolute path from another working directory
/path/to/deploy/@PROJECT_NAME@/@PROJECT_NAME@

# Override the app root explicitly
QORNIX_APP_ROOT=/path/to/deploy/@PROJECT_NAME@ /path/to/@PROJECT_NAME@
```

The deployment bundle includes Qornix application assets, HTML templates, CSS/JS, docs, the demo XML schema and the local `schema_app.xsd`. It does not bundle system shared libraries. If you move the bundle to another machine, make sure required runtime libraries such as Boost, SQLite, yaml-cpp, pugixml, libxml2 and libstdc++ are available there, or build/link them according to your packaging policy. Use `ldd ./@PROJECT_NAME@` on Linux to inspect runtime dependencies.

Manual deploy target:

```bash
cmake --build . --target @PROJECT_NAME@_deploy
```

Install-style layout is also available:

```bash
cmake --install . --prefix /opt/@PROJECT_NAME@
```

## Runtime Docker image

The template includes `runtime-Dockerfile`, which builds a runtime image around the existing deploy bundle. Build the app first:

```bash
mkdir -p build
cmake -S . -B build
cmake --build build --target @PROJECT_NAME@_deploy
docker build -f runtime-Dockerfile -t @PROJECT_NAME@:runtime .
```

Run with the config embedded in the image:

```bash
docker run --rm -p 8008:8008 @PROJECT_NAME@:runtime
```

The image stores application files in `/app`, data in `/data` and file logs in `/logs`. For persistent SQLite data and logs, use named volumes:

```bash
docker volume create @PROJECT_NAME@_data
docker volume create @PROJECT_NAME@_logs
docker run -d --name @PROJECT_NAME@ \
  -p 8008:8008 \
  -v @PROJECT_NAME@_data:/data \
  -v @PROJECT_NAME@_logs:/logs \
  @PROJECT_NAME@:runtime
```

To override config, copy the generated config and mount it over `/app/config.yaml`:

```bash
mkdir -p docker/config data logs
cp build/deploy/@PROJECT_NAME@/config.yaml docker/config/config.yaml
```

Use absolute container paths for mutable files:

```yaml
server:
  address: 0.0.0.0
  port: 8008
logging:
  enabled: true
  level: info
  to_file: true
  file_path: /logs/server
database:
  driver: sqlite
  path: /data/app.sqlite3
dynamic_api:
  schema:
    file: /data/schema/app.schema.xml
    exported_file: /data/schema/database.schema.xml
    history_file: /data/schema/schema_history.jsonl
```

Run with bind-mounted config, data and logs:

```bash
sudo chown -R 10001:10001 data logs
docker run -d --name @PROJECT_NAME@ \
  -p 8008:8008 \
  -v "$PWD/docker/config/config.yaml:/app/config.yaml:ro" \
  -v "$PWD/data:/data" \
  -v "$PWD/logs:/logs" \
  @PROJECT_NAME@:runtime
```

The container user is UID/GID `10001` by default. Change it at image build time if your host policy requires another owner:

```bash
docker build -f runtime-Dockerfile \
  --build-arg QORNIX_UID="$(id -u)" \
  --build-arg QORNIX_GID="$(id -g)" \
  -t @PROJECT_NAME@:runtime .
```

To move the app to another computer, either push/pull the image through a registry or export it:

```bash
docker save @PROJECT_NAME@:runtime -o @PROJECT_NAME@-runtime.tar
```

Copy `@PROJECT_NAME@-runtime.tar` plus your external `docker/config/config.yaml` and `data/` directory if you keep state outside the image. On the target machine:

```bash
docker load -i @PROJECT_NAME@-runtime.tar
docker run -d --name @PROJECT_NAME@ -p 8008:8008 \
  -v "$PWD/docker/config/config.yaml:/app/config.yaml:ro" \
  -v "$PWD/data:/data" \
  -v "$PWD/logs:/logs" \
  @PROJECT_NAME@:runtime
```

If you change the build distribution or enable more database drivers, inspect `ldd build/deploy/@PROJECT_NAME@/@PROJECT_NAME@` and adjust the package list in `runtime-Dockerfile`.

## Pages

```text
GET /                    application landing page and first-run wizard
GET /schema-manager      Schema Manager UI for validate/diff/plan/apply
GET /query-builder       visual structured query builder
GET /table               table browser with filters, fields and sorting
GET /table/{table}/{id}  row view with inline update/delete
GET /api-playground      ready-made Dynamic API recipes
GET /docs                lightweight OpenAPI viewer
GET /docs/schema         human-readable Schema Manager and XML schema guide
GET /docs/query-syntax   human-readable Dynamic API query contract
GET /openapi.json        raw OpenAPI document
```

## Recommended first run

1. Open `/`.
2. Click `Initialize demo database`, or open `/schema-manager`, click `Load demo schema`, then run `Validate -> Diff -> Plan -> Apply live plan`.
3. Click `Initialize demo data`.
4. Open `/query-builder` and build a structured request.
5. Open `/table`, choose `products`, and click a row id to open Row View.
6. Open `/api-playground` to run copyable frontend recipes.
7. Open `/docs` to inspect generated OpenAPI paths.
8. Open `/docs/schema` to read the XML schema and Schema Manager guide.
9. Open `/docs/query-syntax` to read the text specification for query payloads.

